<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/5th-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Delium</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/424-bce">424 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnesian-war">Peloponnesian War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Delium was fought in 424 BCE near Delium on the Boeotian coast during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnesian-war">Peloponnesian War</a>. The Boeotian army under the Theban commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pagondas">Pagondas</a>, drawn up 25 ranks deep and supported by a surprise cavalry attack, defeated an Athenian army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hippocrates">Hippocrates</a>, who was killed. The philosopher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/socrates">Socrates</a> fought on the Athenian side, and the battle ended Athenian plans to take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boeotia">Boeotia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-delium.jpg" alt="Battle of Delium" width="320" height="259" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Thomas Couture · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Socrates_at_the_Battle_of_Delium_(Thomas_Couture,_1843).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In 424 BCE the Athenian generals <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demosthenes-general">Demosthenes</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hippocrates">Hippocrates</a> planned a two-pronged invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boeotia">Boeotia</a>. Demosthenes sailed too early and landed at Siphae, where his plans were betrayed by a Phocian named Nicomachus, and he had to withdraw. Hippocrates then arrived with 7,000 Athenian hoplites, 1,000 cavalry and some 10,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metic">metics</a> and other non-citizens, and fortified the temple at Delium in five days. He left a garrison and sent the rest of the army home. Many Boeotians saw no point in attacking a departing enemy, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pagondas">Pagondas</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a> urged them on, knowing the Athenians would use Delium as a base.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The Boeotians had 7,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoplite">hoplites</a>, 1,000 cavalry, 500 peltasts and 10,000 light troops, with the Thebans on the right wing, the men of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haliartus">Haliartus</a>, Coronea and Copiae in the centre, and Thespiae, Tanagra and Orchomenus on the left. The two armies were hidden from each other by a hill, and the Boeotians charged while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hippocrates">Hippocrates</a> was still addressing his men. The longer Athenian line surrounded the Boeotian left, where only the Thespian contingent stood firm, but in circling them some Athenians killed one another by mistake, the first documented incident of friendly fire.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pagondas">Pagondas</a> sent his cavalry to support the left, the Athenians there broke, and when the Theban right also prevailed the Athenian centre fled. About 500 Boeotians and 1,000 Athenians were killed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a> has <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alcibiades">Alcibiades</a> describe <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/socrates">Socrates</a> retreating calmly beside Laches, so plainly ready to defend himself that pursuers left him alone.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The survivors held the fort at Delium and answered a Boeotian herald's demand to leave sacred ground by claiming the land as their own. After two weeks, joined by 2,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoplite">hoplites</a> from Corinth and other allies, the Boeotians used a device that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thucydides">Thucydides</a> describes, apparently a kind of flamethrower, to set the fort alight; about 200 Athenians died and the rest escaped. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demosthenes-general">Demosthenes</a> arrived too late and was defeated near Sicyon. Pagondas's use of deep ranks, reserves, cavalry, skirmishers and changing tactics marked one of the first planned tactical battles in recorded history.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Delium” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>At the <b>Battle of Delium</b> in 424 BCE the Theban commander Pagondas drew up the Thebans 25 ranks deep and used cavalry to break the Athenian line, an early sign of the tactics that later made Thebes famous.</blockquote><a href="/empire/boeotian-league">Boeotian League · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Peloponnesian War the Thebans besieged Plataea in 431 BCE, destroyed it after its capture in 427 BCE and, in 424 BCE, defeated an Athenian army at the <b>Battle of Delium</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/thebes">Thebes · Military</a></li></ul></section>
